# --- this fork ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The Zig build graph here writes nothing into the source tree. Everything it
# generates - the pcre2.h copy, the copied pcre2_chartables.c, the generated
# config.h, the expanded libpcre2-*.sym version scripts, the .pc files and the
# pcre2-config script - is an output of `b.addWriteFiles`,
# `b.addConfigHeader` or a `b.addSystemCommand`, so it lands under the build
# cache directory; the artifacts land in the install prefix. `RunTest`'s own
# `testoutput*` working directories land there too, because `upstream-test`
# runs it with a generated current directory. The four Zig rules above carry no
# leading slash, so they match at any depth and already cover the nested
# consumer package in consumer/ and its own cache and prefix.
#
# The rules that cover generated metadata in a source build are already above
# and are left alone: `*.pc` excludes a configured `.pc`, `pcre2-config`
# excludes a generated one, and `src/libpcre2-*.sym` excludes a configured
# version script. This graph generates all three into the cache instead, so
# none of those paths is ever written here.
#
# Nothing this fork adds to the tree is excluded by any rule above, and none of
# the rules above is removed or narrowed here: they belong to upstream's
# autotools, CMake and Bazel builds, which are still how a source checkout of
# this repository is configured, and this fork replaces none of them.
#
# The negations below name the fork's own sources for that reason only. They are
# a no-op today; they exist because this file is upstream's, is shared with every
# other PCRE2 checkout, and the normal way a file goes missing from a package is
# an upstream rule broadening to cover it. A negation cannot re-include a file
# whose parent directory is excluded, so each directory is named as well as its
# contents.
#
# The last five negations name the upstream templates build.zig `@embedFile`s.
# None of them is excluded by a rule above today, and they are listed anyway:
# `*.pc` is a suffix rule that a future upstream broadening could turn into a
# prefix or substring rule, and a template that quietly vanished from a package
# would cost the whole pkg-config closure rather than one file.
!translate.h
!cross-test.profile.sh
!/zig/
!/tests.zig
!/smoke.zig
!/consumer/
!/testdata/
!pcre2-config.in
!libpcre2-8.pc.in
!libpcre2-16.pc.in
!libpcre2-32.pc.in
!libpcre2-posix.pc.in

# End
